Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, naturalistic faces and fabric, candlelit 1840s interiors, deep shadow.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

A sparse bedroom in an old house. Heavy wooden shutters fastened tight. An OLD MAN—elderly, thin, white hair, pale skin, nightshirt—sleeps in a high wooden bed. One lid shows a pale blue eye filmed like a vulture's.

(SOUND: slow clock tick from somewhere in the house, floorboards settling)

In the doorway shadow stands the NARRATOR—middle years, gaunt pale face, dark hollow eyes, black waistcoat over a white shirt, sleeves rolled, hands faintly trembling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

He tilts his head, listening to the dark.

(SOUND: a faint high ringing under the clock)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them. Above all was the sense of hearing acute.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

A narrow unlit hall. The NARRATOR faces the chamber door, a dark lantern closed in his hands. His face is intent, almost gentle.

(SOUND: latch barely whispering)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It is impossible to say how first the idea entered my brain; but once conceived, it haunted me day and night.

He rests his palm on the wood, breathing slow.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Object there was none. Passion there was none. I loved the old man. He had never wronged me.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Moonless dark. The OLD MAN sleeps. The pale blue filmed eye is shut.

(SOUND: soft distant wind at the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

A thin memory of that eye—open, dull blue, veiled—flashes in the dark, then gone.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it. Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

The NARRATOR, same gaunt face and black waistcoat, turns the latch with exquisite care. He opens the door a hand's width and slides the closed dark lantern through.

(SOUND: hinges breathing a tiny creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So by degrees—very gradually—I made up my mind to take the life of the old man, and thus rid myself of the eye forever.

He eases his head into the opening, moving as slow as a minute hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
You fancy me mad. Madmen know nothing. But you should have seen me. You should have seen how wisely I proceeded.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

From the cracked door, a single thin ray of lantern light crawls across the bedclothes and stops on the OLD MAN's closed eye.

(SOUND: lantern tin faintly ticking as it steadies)

The NARRATOR's eye gleams in the ray's edge. He watches. The eye does not open.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

He holds motionless a long beat, then draws back. The ray dies.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
This I did for seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed; and so it was impossible to do the work.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - DAY

Morning light seeps at the shutter edges. The NARRATOR stands boldly inside, hearty posture, same pale face now arranged in kindness. The OLD MAN sits up in bed, thin hands on the coverlet, the filmed pale blue eye half open.

(SOUND: shutters ticking in a small draft, birds faint outside)

NARRATOR
And how have you passed the night?

The OLD MAN nods, unsuspecting. The NARRATOR smiles, warm and steady.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every morning, when the day broke, I went boldly into the chamber, and spoke courageously to him, calling him by name in a hearty tone.

INT. HALL OUTSIDE CHAMBER - NIGHT

Midnight. The NARRATOR at the door again—gaunt, black waistcoat, dark lantern closed. His fingers move slower than a watch's minute hand. A tight smile of triumph.

(SOUND: latch easing, almost no sound at all)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Upon the eighth night I was more than usually cautious in opening the door. Never before that night had I felt the extent of my own powers—of my sagacity.

He chuckles under his breath—barely a breath.

(SOUND: a sudden rustle of bedclothes within)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I fairly chuckled at the idea; and perhaps he heard me; for he moved on the bed suddenly, as if startled.

He does not draw back. He pushes the door on, steadily, steadily, into pitch black.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Thick darkness. Shutters close fastened. The NARRATOR's head is inside. His thumb slips on the tin fastening of the lantern.

(SOUND: sharp tin click)

The OLD MAN springs up in the bed—a pale shape in the dark.

OLD MAN
Who's there?

Silence. The NARRATOR freezes in the opening, not a muscle. The OLD MAN remains sitting up, listening.

(SOUND: deathwatch beetles ticking faintly in the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I kept quite still and said nothing. For a whole hour I did not move a muscle, and in the meantime I did not hear him lie down.

A long held beat. The OLD MAN's outline stays upright.

(SOUND: a slight stifled groan)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Presently I heard a slight groan, and I knew it was the groan of mortal terror. I knew the sound well.

The NARRATOR's mouth twitches—pity and a private chuckle.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Still the standoff in black. The NARRATOR's fingers open a very little crevice in the lantern.

(SOUND: tin easing, a thread of metal scrape)

A single dim ray, thin as spider-thread, shoots out and falls full upon the vulture eye.

The eye is open—wide, wide open—dull blue with a hideous veil.

(SOUND: the NARRATOR's breath catching)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it. I saw it with perfect distinctness—all a dull blue, with a hideous veil over it.

Nothing else of the face is lit. Only the damned spot.

(SOUND: a low, dull, quick sound—as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton)

The NARRATOR's eyes widen. He holds the lantern motionless. He scarcely breathes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound. I knew that sound well, too. It was the beating of the old man's heart.

(SOUND: the muffled heartbeat growing quicker, louder)

He keeps still. The ray shakes once, then steadies on the eye.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It increased my fury, as the beating of a drum stimulates the soldier into courage. Meantime the hellish tattoo of the heart increased.

(SOUND: heartbeat louder still under the dead-hour silence of the house)

Sweat stands on the NARRATOR's brow. His free hand clenches.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew louder, I say, louder every moment!—do you mark me well? I thought the heart must burst.

(SOUND: heartbeat pounding toward the walls)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now a new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour! The old man's hour had come!

With a loud yell he throws the lantern full open and leaps into the room. Light slashes the walls.

(SOUND: yell ripping the dark; bed ropes shrieking)

The OLD MAN shrieks once—once only. The NARRATOR drags him to the floor and pulls the heavy bed over him.

(MUSIC: a harsh dissonant surge, then cut to muffled pulse)

The NARRATOR stands over the overturned bed, smiling gaily, chest heaving.

(SOUND: the heart still beating on, muffled, many minutes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I then smiled gaily, to find the deed so far done. But, for many minutes, the heart beat on with a muffled sound.

He waits, head cocked. The muffled beat slows.

(SOUND: heartbeat weakening, then silence)

He removes the bed. Kneels. Places his hand upon the heart and holds it there.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead. Yes, he was stone, stone dead. His eye would trouble me no more.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Later. A tub stands by the bed. The NARRATOR works in silence—same gaunt face, now smeared and intent—dismembering what the camera only implies in shadow and the motion of a saw and cloth.

(SOUND: wet cloth, careful saw strokes, no voice)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If still you think me mad, you will think so no longer when I describe the wise precautions I took for the concealment of the body.

He pries up three planks from the flooring, deposits the wrapped remains between the scantlings, and replaces the boards with cunning care. He runs his palm over the seams—seamless.

(SOUND: planks seating home, a soft satisfied pat)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected any thing wrong. A tub had caught all—ha! ha!

He wipes his hands. No stain on the floor.

(SOUND: a distant bell striking four)

INT. STREET DOOR - NIGHT

Still dark as midnight. Knocking at the street door. The NARRATOR descends with a light heart, lantern raised, black waistcoat straightened, pale face composed to welcome.

(SOUND: knocking firm and official; bolt drawn)

Three men enter—OFFICER REYNOLDS (bearded, cape, calm authority), OFFICER HALE (younger, sharp eyes), OFFICER BRIGGS (broad, notebook in hand)—hats in hand, perfect suavity.

(SOUND: night air, boots on threshold)

OFFICER REYNOLDS
A shriek was heard by a neighbour. We are to search the premises.

The NARRATOR smiles—what has he now to fear?

NARRATOR
Gentlemen—welcome. The shriek was my own, in a dream. The old man is absent in the country.

He bows them in.

INT. OLD MAN'S CHAMBER - NIGHT

Lamp light. The NARRATOR leads OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HALE, and OFFICER BRIGGS through the chamber. He shows the old man's treasures—secure, undisturbed. In wild audacity he brings chairs and seats the officers. He places his own chair upon the very planks that hide the corpse.

(SOUND: chairs scraping; pleasant settled quiet)

NARRATOR
Search—search well. Rest from your fatigues.

He sits atop the hidden place, singularly at ease, answering cheerily. The officers chat of familiar things in low agreeable tones—no distinct invented banter, only the murmur of ease.

(SOUND: easy male murmuring, a chair creak)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them. I was singularly at ease.

Ere long his face pales. He touches his temple.

(SOUND: a faint ringing rising under the chat)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone. My head ached, and I fancied a ringing in my ears.

He talks more freely, smile stretched. The officers still sit, still smile.

(SOUND: ringing growing distinct—then resolving into a low, dull, quick beat, as a watch in cotton)

His eyes flick to the floorboards beneath his chair.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears. It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

He gasps once for breath. The officers do not react. He talks more quickly, more vehemently, hands cutting the air.

(SOUND: heartbeat steady under his raised voice)

He rises. Argues about trifles in a high key, violent gesticulations. Paces the floor with heavy strides.

(SOUND: boots pounding boards; heartbeat louder)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone? Oh God! what could I do?

He foams, raves, swears under breath. Grabs his chair and grates it hard upon the boards.

(SOUND: chair legs screaming on wood—yet the heartbeat rises over all)

(MUSIC: tight escalating drone under the beat)

Louder—louder—louder. OFFICER REYNOLDS, OFFICER HALE, and OFFICER BRIGGS chat on pleasantly and smile.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Was it possible they heard not? Almighty God!—no, no! They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!

His face breaks. He cannot bear the smiles.

(SOUND: heartbeat thundering; his breath tearing)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I felt that I must scream or die! and now—again!—hark! louder! louder! louder! louder!

He shrieks, pointing at the floor beneath them.

NARRATOR
Villains! Dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

(SOUND: the heartbeat slamming once more—then his sob of air)

The officers spring up. The NARRATOR still points at the boards, eyes wild, the pale gaunt face fixed in ruin.

(SOUND: chair toppling; the dull quick beat continuing under all)

FADE OUT.

THE END
